---
import CaseLayout from '../../layouts/CaseLayout.astro';
import Diagram from '../../components/Diagram.astro';
import MethodDiagram from '../../components/visuals/MethodDiagram.astro';

const sections = [
  { id: 'thesis', label: 'The thesis' },
  { id: 'why-now', label: 'Why now' },
  { id: 'what', label: 'What it is' },
  { id: 'method', label: 'The method' },
  { id: 'status', label: 'Status' },
  { id: 'questions', label: 'Open questions' },
];
---

<CaseLayout slug="service-and-standard" sections={sections}>
  <section id="thesis">
    <h2><span class="n">01 / The thesis</span>The business knows more than it has written down.</h2>
    <p>
      Not the mission statement. The operating knowledge: the order the close has to happen in,
      which supplier to call when the first one is out, what &ldquo;done&rdquo; actually looks like,
      the workaround for the machine that&rsquo;s been half-broken since 2019, the reason you
      don&rsquo;t schedule that task on a Friday.
    </p>
    <p>
      It works. It has worked for years. It keeps working right until someone leaves, or a second
      location opens and the standard doesn&rsquo;t travel with it, or ownership changes and the new
      owner finds out they bought a building and a name rather than a system.
    </p>
    <p class="pull">
      Then the business pays real money to re-learn things it already knew.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="why-now">
    <h2><span class="n">02 / Why now</span>The capture was always the expensive part.</h2>
    <p>
      &ldquo;Write it all down&rdquo; has always been the right answer. It was just never
      affordable. Somebody has to shadow the work, ask the questions, draft the procedures, reconcile
      the contradictions, keep it current and translate it &mdash; weeks of attention from a person
      who does not have weeks.
    </p>
    <p>
      That arithmetic has changed. A recorded walkthrough can become a first-draft procedure. A
      decade of scattered notes, labels and messages can be sorted into something with a shape. A
      second language version can come out of the same pass instead of being a separate project
      nobody funds.
    </p>
    <p>
      The judgement stays human. Deciding which of three versions of a process is the correct one is
      not a model&rsquo;s job &mdash; it&rsquo;s the owner&rsquo;s, and it&rsquo;s the most valuable
      hour in the engagement. But the labour that used to make this uneconomical for a business with
      fifteen employees is now small.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="what">
    <h2><span class="n">03 / What it is</span>One operations system, built for one business.</h2>
    <p>
      The same shape as the portal I built for the shop I work in, made repeatable: SOPs, recipes and
      builds, training and onboarding, checklists, equipment guides, troubleshooting, cleaning
      standards, policies and a manager layer &mdash; searchable, visual, multilingual, and
      delivered on the phone people already carry rather than in a binder on a shelf.
    </p>
    <p>
      Not a template anyone logs into. A system that describes one specific business, because a
      generic SOP library is exactly the thing everybody already ignores.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="method">
    <h2><span class="n">04 / The method</span>Shadow, capture, structure, build, hand over.</h2>

    <Diagram caption="Five stages, and the thing each one has to leave behind.">
      <MethodDiagram />
    </Diagram>

    <ul class="rows">
      <li>
        <b>Shadow</b>
        Work the shift, or get as close to it as they&rsquo;ll allow. You cannot document a process
        from a conference room. The gap between the written procedure and the real one is where all
        of the value is hiding.
      </li>
      <li>
        <b>Capture</b>
        Interviews with the people who actually know, recordings of the work being done correctly,
        photographs of the right result, and every artefact that already exists &mdash; the binder,
        the shelf labels, the sticky note taped to the machine.
      </li>
      <li>
        <b>Structure</b>
        Decide what&rsquo;s a procedure, what&rsquo;s a reference, what&rsquo;s a decision tree and
        what&rsquo;s a checklist. Surface the contradictions and make somebody choose. This is the
        step that quietly improves the operation.
      </li>
      <li>
        <b>Build</b>
        Put it on a phone. Search first, visual wherever visual is faster, short enough to act on
        mid-task. Written for the person doing the job.
      </li>
      <li>
        <b>Hand over</b>
        It&rsquo;s worthless the moment it goes stale. Somebody inside the business has to own it,
        be able to change it without me, and know when a change is needed. If that person
        doesn&rsquo;t exist, the project doesn&rsquo;t either.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="status">
    <h2><span class="n">05 / Status</span>An idea with one working proof.</h2>
    <div class="note">
      <p class="label">Concept</p>
      <p>
        The B&rsquo;s Bagels portal is the evidence the shape works: it exists, I built it, and it
        was built inside a real operation rather than for a hypothetical one. Service &amp; Standard
        is the attempt to make that repeatable somewhere I don&rsquo;t already work &mdash; which is
        a genuinely different problem, because I won&rsquo;t have years of context and will have to
        get it in days. No clients, no revenue, no launch date. If that changes, this page changes.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="questions">
    <h2><span class="n">06 / Open questions</span>What I&rsquo;d want to find out first.</h2>
    <p>
      I&rsquo;d rather write down the things I don&rsquo;t know than pretend the model is finished.
    </p>
    <ul class="bullets">
      <li>
        Can I get to a usable draft of an operation I&rsquo;ve never worked in, in under two weeks?
      </li>
      <li>
        Who inside a fifteen-person business genuinely owns keeping this current &mdash; and will
        they, once the novelty is gone?
      </li>
      <li>
        Does a business value this <em>before</em> the crisis, or only in the month after someone
        important quits?
      </li>
      <li>
        Is the unit of sale the system, or the ongoing maintenance of it? The second is more useful
        and much harder to sell.
      </li>
      <li>
        How much of this generalises past food service before it stops being the same product?
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>
</CaseLayout>
